 "notes": "28 stations, 4–29 plus 26B and 27B. The list sits under \"EAST OF SCOTLAND.\" with three sub-headings: Shetland, Orkney and Mainland. I used the sub-headings as coast. For the Shetland and Orkney rows I left place null, because the island group is the heading and isn't printed in the row.\n\nHow the columns were split:\n- The column headed \"Returns in 6 years.\" is a count. It goes first in other, followed by the value (Roman numeral, under \"Values.\") and then the height (under \"Feet.\"). They are separated by \"; \".\n- An asterisk in the \"In 1885.\" column is recorded in marks as \"*\" (a return in 1885).\n- A blank count column is left out of other. So a row with only \"V.; 65\" has no count printed.\n- Numbers are printed with a full stop (\"4.\", \"26B.\"). I give them without it.\n- Names keep the trailing comma as printed. For rows that also print a county or locality, the name is the part before the first comma and the rest goes in place, with the trailing comma dropped.\n- No keepers or light characters are printed in this list.\n- Fragments are the full printed lines, leading spaces included.\n\nOdd rows:\n- Start Point (9) has an asterisk for 1885 but nothing in the 6-year column, although an 1885 return should count towards the 6 years. This may be an omission in the print or the transcription.\n- 26B Carr Rock: the whole of \"Carr Rock Light-ship\" is in italics (underscores). I split it into name \"Carr Rock\" and type \"Light-ship\", and gave marks as \"italic; †\". Its value/height column holds just \"?\", which I put in other.\n- 27B Fidra has \"1st\" in the 6-year column, apparently meaning first year. It has no asterisk, no value and no height, and carries the † for a new station.\n- The † footnote \"New Stations, 1885-86.\" (line 574) applies to 26B and 27B.\n\nSpellings and places as printed, kept but worth checking:\n- \"Whalsey Skerries\" (usually Whalsay).\n- \"N. Ronaldshay\".\n- \"Tarbat Head\" (the lighthouse is usually Tarbat Ness).\n- \"Montroseness\" and \"Girdleness\" are each one word.\n- \"Chanonry Point, Elgin\": Chanonry is in Ross-shire, so \"Elgin\" looks like a slip, possibly carried over from Covesea Skerries on the next line. Your last commit notes another slip for Chanonry Point, in Findlay 1879.\n- \"Cromarty, East Cromarty\".\n- \"Bell Rock, Fife Coast\": the Bell Rock is off Forfarshire, so this is probably just the committee's grouping.\n\nThe lead-in's mention of \"migration values\" refers to the Values column (I.–V.), which was explained in the previous report.",
